POSTS={
"mold-behind-drywall-how-to-tell":{
"title":"How to Tell If Mold Is Growing Behind Drywall",
"desc":"Mold behind drywall shows up as staining, soft board and a musty smell. Here is how to confirm it without tearing the wall apart.",
"h1":"How to Tell If Mold Is Growing Behind Your Drywall",
"eyebrow":"Mold","img":"dehumidifier-kitchen-water-damage-largo.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Mold behind drywall is common in Largo homes because the back of the board stays wet long after the painted face feels dry. The paper facing on drywall is food, the cavity holds moisture, and nothing in the room shows it until staining or odour appears.",
 "You can usually narrow it down without opening anything. The signs are specific, and the tools used to find the moisture are non destructive."],
"sections":[
 ("The Signs That Point Inside the Wall",[
  ("ul",["A musty smell strongest at floor level or in a closet",
         "A dark or shadowed line along the wall just above the baseboard",
         "Paint bubbling, blistering or peeling in a band rather than a patch",
         "Drywall that feels soft or gives slightly under thumb pressure",
         "Baseboard separating from the wall or cupping",
         "Tape seams lifting at the bottom of the wall"]),
  "A band of damage near the floor is the classic pattern. Water travels sideways across a slab, reaches the wall, then wicks upward through the board, which is why the line sits a few inches to a foot above the floor."]),
 ("Where to Check First in a Largo House",[
  ("table",["Location","Why it is likely"],
   [["Wall base after any past water loss","Wicking leaves the bottom of the board wet"],
    ["Shared wall with a bathroom","Supply and drain lines plus constant humidity"],
    ["Air handler closet wall","Condensate overflow soaks the wall and the room behind it"],
    ["Exterior wall behind furniture","Still air plus a cool surface produces condensation"],
    ["Under window sills","Failed seals let wind driven rain into the cavity"],
    ["Kitchen wall behind the dishwasher","Slow supply or drain leaks run for months unnoticed"]])]),
 ("Non Destructive Tools That Find It",[
  "A pinless moisture meter reads moisture content through the surface without marking it. Sweeping a wall with one shows where readings rise, which maps the wet area.",
  "Thermal imaging shows surface temperature differences. Evaporating moisture cools a surface, so a wet area typically appears as a cooler region behind the paint. The camera does not see mold, it sees the conditions mold needs.",
  "Used together these two tools usually locate the problem within a small area, which keeps any inspection opening small."]),
 ("Confirming It Means a Small Opening",[
  "Meters and cameras find moisture. Confirming actual growth behind the board requires looking, which means a small inspection cut, usually a few inches, made low on the wall where it is easy to repair.",
  "A borescope through a drilled hole is a smaller alternative when the wall finish is expensive or historic."]),
 ("What Not to Do",[
  "Do not paint over it. Sealing the surface traps moisture in the cavity and the staining returns through the new coat.",
  "Do not spray bleach on the drywall. It adds water to porous material without reaching growth on the back of the board.",
  "Do not open a large area before the source is identified. The demolition is only useful once you know where the water came from."]),
 ("What Happens If It Is Confirmed",[
  "Containment goes up, the moisture source is corrected first, affected porous material is removed and bagged, remaining surfaces are HEPA vacuumed and wiped, and the cavity is dried to a standard before anything is closed back up.",
  "Florida separates mold assessment from remediation, so a project that needs a written protocol starts with an independent assessor."])],
"guides_h2":"Related Mold Guides",
"svcblurbs":["Containment, HEPA filtration, removal and verification.",
 "Moisture mapping, thermal imaging and sampling with a written report."],
"faq_h2":"Mold Behind Drywall: Common Questions",
"faqs":[
 ("How can I tell if mold is behind my drywall?","Look for a musty smell, a dark band above the baseboard, bubbling paint and board that feels soft. A pinless meter confirms whether the area is wet."),
 ("Can mold be found without cutting the wall?","Moisture meters and thermal imaging locate the wet area without damage. Confirming growth on the back of the board still takes a small inspection opening."),
 ("Why is the damage always near the floor?","Water spreads sideways across a slab, reaches the wall and wicks upward through the drywall, which leaves a band a few inches to a foot above the floor."),
 ("Does thermal imaging see mold?","No. It shows temperature differences caused by evaporating moisture, which identifies where conditions support growth. It is a locating tool, not a diagnosis."),
 ("Can I just paint over the stain?","No. Painting seals moisture into the cavity and the staining comes back through the new coat. The moisture source has to be corrected first."),
 ("Will bleach fix mold on drywall?","No. Drywall is porous, so bleach adds water near the surface without reaching growth on the back of the board, and the problem often returns."),
 ("How much wall has to come out?","Only what the readings require, usually a band cut to a straight line above the wick line. Cutting to the height the moisture reached keeps the rebuild smaller."),
 ("Is mold behind drywall dangerous?","It is a health concern mainly for people with asthma, allergies or immune conditions, and it damages material over time. It should be removed rather than sealed in."),
 ("How long does mold take to grow in a wall?","In Florida humidity, growth can begin on wet porous material within about twenty four to forty eight hours of the cavity getting wet."),
 ("Do I need an independent mold assessor?","For projects needing a written protocol and clearance, yes. Florida generally prevents one company from both assessing and remediating the same job.")]},

"what-is-a-dry-standard":{
"title":"What a Dry Standard Is and How It Is Set",
"desc":"A dry standard is a reading taken from unaffected material in your own building. Here is how it is set and why it ends the job.",
"h1":"What a Dry Standard Is and How It Is Set",
"eyebrow":"Drying science","img":"drying-equipment-staged-largo-garage.jpg",
"intro":[
 "A dry standard is the moisture reading a water damage job has to reach before equipment comes out. It is taken from the same material in an unaffected part of your building, which makes it specific to your house rather than a number copied from a chart.",
 "Without one, drying ends when somebody decides the room feels dry. That is the single most common reason a repaired room smells six weeks later."],
"sections":[
 ("How the Standard Is Established",[
  "On the first visit, readings are taken from material of the same type in a part of the building the water never reached. Drywall is compared to drywall, subfloor to subfloor, framing to framing.",
  "Those readings become the target. The wet areas are dried until they match, which accounts for the natural moisture a building carries in this climate.",
  ("table",["Material","Where the baseline is taken"],
   [["Drywall","Same wall type in a dry room, at the same height"],
    ["Subfloor or slab","An unaffected area of the same floor assembly"],
    ["Framing and bottom plate","Dry framing in the same structure"],
    ["Hardwood","Boards from the same floor outside the wet zone"]])]),
 ("Why a Fixed Number Does Not Work",[
  "A 1960s concrete block house in Largo and a 2015 frame house do not carry the same baseline moisture, and neither matches a textbook figure.",
  "Florida changes this further. Materials here sit at higher equilibrium moisture content than the same materials in a dry climate, so a number that signals a problem elsewhere can be entirely normal in Pinellas County."]),
 ("What Gets Recorded",[
  ("ul",["Baseline readings by material on day one",
         "Readings from each affected area, in the same spots, every visit",
         "Temperature and relative humidity inside the drying chamber",
         "Grain depression across the dehumidifier",
         "Equipment counts and placement changes"]),
  "That record is also the document your adjuster evaluates. Daily readings showing progress toward a baseline carry more weight than a description of how wet it was."]),
 ("When the Readings Stall",[
  "If material stops improving, something is wrong with the plan rather than with the building. Common causes are a cavity that air is not reaching, a source that was never fully stopped, or material too saturated to recover.",
  "At that point the options are to change equipment placement, open a cavity for direct access, or remove the material. Running the same setup for more days rarely helps."]),
 ("How the Job Closes",[
  "Final readings are taken at the same points used throughout, and they have to match the baseline. Equipment is then removed and a completion record is issued showing the readings on the final day.",
  "Ask for that record. It is what proves the structure was dry before anything was closed up, and it is the document you want if a question arises later."])],
"guides_h2":"More on Drying and Equipment",
"svcblurbs":["Calculated loads, daily logs and verified dry standards.",
 "Extraction before drying, with the water line documented."],
"faq_h2":"Dry Standard Questions From Largo Owners",
"faqs":[
 ("What is a dry standard?","A moisture reading taken from unaffected material in your own building. Wet areas are dried until they match it, which makes the target specific to your house."),
 ("Why not use a fixed moisture percentage?","Because buildings differ. A 1960s block home and a new frame home carry different baseline moisture, and Florida materials sit higher than the same materials elsewhere."),
 ("Who decides when drying is finished?","The readings do. The job closes when the affected materials match the baseline taken from unaffected material of the same type."),
 ("How often are readings taken?","Every visit, usually daily, from the same points each time so progress can be compared rather than estimated."),
 ("What if the readings stop improving?","That means the plan needs changing, not more days. Usually a cavity is not getting air, the source was not fully stopped, or material is too saturated to recover."),
 ("Should I get a copy of the readings?","Yes. The log showing baseline, daily readings and final figures is what proves the structure was dry before it was closed up."),
 ("Can a room feel dry but fail the standard?","Frequently. Surfaces dry first while the wall base, cavity insulation and subfloor still hold water a meter can detect."),
 ("Does the standard change between materials?","Yes. Drywall is compared to drywall, subfloor to subfloor and framing to framing, because each material holds moisture differently."),
 ("Does my adjuster need the dry standard?","It helps. A log showing daily movement toward a baseline is stronger evidence than a description of the damage."),
 ("How long until materials reach the standard?","Most residential losses take three to five days. Block walls, tile over slab and hardwood commonly take longer.")]},

"laminate-and-vinyl-plank-water":{
"title":"Laminate and Vinyl Plank After Water: What Survives",
"desc":"Laminate rarely survives a soaking. Vinyl plank often does, but the slab underneath still has to be dried. Here is the difference.",
"h1":"Laminate and Vinyl Plank After Water: What Survives",
"eyebrow":"Materials and salvage","img":"tile-floor-injection-drying-largo-home.jpg",
"intro":[
 "Laminate and vinyl plank look similar on a Largo floor and behave completely differently when water gets under them. Laminate has a fibreboard core that swells permanently. Vinyl plank is waterproof as a material, which creates a different problem entirely.",
 "In both cases the flooring is only half the question. What sits underneath, and whether it can dry with the floor still down, decides the job."],
"sections":[
 ("The Core Is What Decides",[
  ("table",["","Laminate","Luxury vinyl plank","Engineered wood"],
   [["Core material","High density fibreboard","PVC or composite","Plywood"],
    ["Reaction to water","Swells, irreversible","Unaffected","Delaminates"],
    ["Edge damage","Lifts and chips at joints","Usually none","Layers separate"],
    ["Typical outcome","Replacement","Often reusable","Usually replacement"]])]),
 ("Why Laminate Rarely Survives",[
  "The fibreboard core absorbs water and expands. The expansion is permanent, so even a floor that dries out keeps swollen edges and lifted joints.",
  "Damage shows first at the plank ends and along seams, where the core is exposed under the wear layer. Once those edges have risen, sanding or weighting does not bring them back."]),
 ("Why Waterproof Flooring Is Not a Waterproof Floor",[
  "Vinyl plank does not absorb water, and that is exactly the problem. Water that gets under it cannot evaporate upward through the plank, so it sits on the slab or subfloor underneath.",
  "On a Largo slab, that trapped water spreads sideways and wicks into wall bases while the floor surface looks perfectly normal. Owners often discover it through a smell rather than a sight.",
  "Click lock vinyl can sometimes be lifted carefully, the slab dried, and the same planks reinstalled. Glue down vinyl usually comes up in pieces and is replaced."]),
 ("How Crews Decide",[
  ("ul",["Meter readings at the slab or subfloor, not just the surface",
         "Whether the floor is click lock or glue down",
         "How long the water sat before extraction",
         "Whether the water was clean or contaminated",
         "Whether matching replacement material is still available"]),
  "Contaminated water ends the discussion. Category three water under any floating floor means removal, because the assembly cannot be cleaned in place."]),
 ("What Drying Looks Like Under a Floating Floor",[
  "Small sections are lifted at the perimeter to give air access to the subfloor. In some cases planks are removed in a strip along one wall, the assembly is dried, then the strip is reinstalled.",
  "Where the floor cannot be lifted without destroying it, the choice is between removing it and leaving moisture in the building. Leaving it is how mold starts under a floor that still looks new."]),
 ("Replacing Like for Like",[
  "Discontinued patterns are a frequent complication. Insurers generally pay for a reasonable match rather than replacing undamaged flooring in adjacent rooms, which is worth raising with your adjuster early if the floor runs continuously through the house."])],
"guides_h2":"Related Materials and Salvage Guides",
"svcblurbs":["Extraction, pad replacement and in place drying for soft flooring.",
 "Mat systems and controlled drying for wood floor assemblies."],
"faq_h2":"Laminate and Vinyl Plank Water Questions",
"faqs":[
 ("Can laminate flooring be saved after water damage?","Rarely. The fibreboard core swells permanently once wet, and swollen edges and lifted joints do not return to shape as the floor dries."),
 ("Is vinyl plank waterproof?","The plank material is, but the floor is not. Water gets under it through seams and edges and then cannot evaporate upward, so it sits on the slab."),
 ("Can vinyl plank be lifted and reused?","Click lock plank often can, if it is removed carefully and the slab underneath is dried. Glue down vinyl usually comes up in pieces."),
 ("How do I know if water got under my floating floor?","Meter readings at the perimeter and along seams, plus a musty smell or soft spots. Surface appearance is not a reliable guide."),
 ("What happens if water is left under vinyl plank?","It spreads along the slab and wicks into wall bases. Microbial growth follows, which is why floors that look new can still smell."),
 ("Does engineered wood survive water better than laminate?","Only slightly. Once water reaches the plywood core the layers delaminate, and delaminated plank does not recover."),
 ("Will my insurer replace the whole floor?","Usually they pay for a reasonable match in the affected area rather than replacing undamaged flooring elsewhere. Raise continuity with your adjuster early."),
 ("How long before water under a floor causes problems?","In Florida humidity, microbial growth can begin on wet porous material within about two days, so early extraction matters more than the volume of water."),
 ("Can a dehumidifier dry a floor without lifting it?","Not usually. Water trapped under a non permeable plank has no path upward, so sections normally have to be lifted to give air access."),
 ("Should I replace with something more water tolerant?","Tile over slab and true waterproof plank handle spills better, though any floating floor still traps water underneath if it gets in.")]},
}
